ystem is the user clicking on the  link that they shouldn't it's really  hard to defend against that because what  you have is a user authorizing whoever's  in control of that link they just click  to do stuff to their computer you know  the cyber security industry has really  focused on you know protecting with what  experts called the perimeter which is  you know protecting attackers from kind  of brute forcing their way through your  network we're actually pretty good at  that not perfect but you know the US and  other countries are actually pretty  decent at doing that right now the  problem always becomes what hap  when you have somebody inside the  network doing something they shouldn't  and not because they're knowingly  infecting their network in the case of  the DNC hack it was Hillary Clinton's  you know campaign manager John Podesta  clicking on a google password reset link  that was a malicious link really hard to  protect the gifts Google does its best  to try to flag users when you know  things like that happen but you know  what winds up being some of the best  protection you know winds up being using  two-factor authentication which are text  message alerts to your phone hey is this  really you logging into this account if  so enter this code and that's not a  perfect protection but it's far better  than having none at all and before we  get there last question here you know I  wanted to show one other piece of  information that's come up in regard to  this Germany election issue at the  beginning of december WikiLeaks which  published a lot of the leaked Clinton  emails publish this this was a huge file  of information you know on the German  parliament and specifically it 90  gigabytes of data it's a lot of data 90  gigabytes of data of information on the  German parliament specifically the  German Parliament's investigation into  collusion or cooperation between Germany  and the NSA Germans the Germany spy  chief came out and publicly blamed you  